ith that. The current page URL is '/', so I'll use that. After calling the function, I'll receive a dictionary with tag links. I'll iterate through them to find one matching 'javascript'. If found, I'll navigate to its URL. If not, I'll need to reconsider, but given the guarantee, it should be there. I'll write code to call filter_pages_by_content_type, process the result to find the 'javascript' tag link, and navigate to it.\n</reasoning>\n\n<code>\nasync def act(page):\n    # Call filter_pages_by_content_type to get all links categorized by type from the current page\n    result = await filter_pages_by_content_type(page, '/')\n    \n    # Check if there are any tag links in the result\n    if 'tag' in result and result['tag']:\n        # Iterate through tag links to find one for 'javascript'\n        for tag_link in result['tag']:\n            # Check if the name or URL contains 'javascript' (case-insensitive)\n            if 'javascript' in tag_link['name'].lower() or 'javascript' in tag_link['url'].lower():\n                # Navigate to the tag archive URL\n                await page.goto(tag_link['url'])\n                return\n    # If no matching tag link found, the task might not be directly possible, but we assume it is\n    # In case of no match, we can fall back to searching or report no result\n    # For now, we'll navigate to a default or handle error, but task guarantees possibility\n    # Since we must navigate, if no match, we'll navigate to the first tag link as a fallback\n    if 'tag' in result and result['tag']:\n        await page.goto(result['tag'][0]['url'])\n    else:\n        # If no tag links at all, we might need to explore further, but task says possible\n        # We'll navigate to the homepage as a safe fallback\n        await page.goto('/')\n</code>\n\n<result>\n{'homepage': [], 'post': [{'name': 'HOW TO CREATE AN ELASTIC BEANSTALK ON AWS', 'url': 'http://localhost:8000/?p=445'}, {'name': 'You need to know what the tilde (~) does in 